## Results and discussion
As a representative demonstration, the structure of cMIL-125@ZIF-8 heterostructure with ZIF-8 grown on the corner of $\mathtt{c M l L-125}$ was firstly investigated. $\mathsf{c M l L-}125$ particles synthesized via a solvothermal method43 (see experimental section) were used as the host. As shown in the field-emission scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images (Figure S1a, b), $\mathtt{c M l L-}125$ are highly dispersed with an average length of $\sim650\ \mathsf{n m}$ and thickness of ${\sim}175\ \mathsf{n m}$ . Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images (Figure S1c) show the solid nature of these cake-like particles. X-ray diffraction (XRD) pattern confirms the high crystallinity of $\mathsf{c M l L-}125$ (Figure S2), in agreement with the structure reported in literature.43
Subsequently, ZIF-8 nanocrystals were directly grown onto $\mathsf{c M l L-}125$ without using surfactants, forming MOF-on-MOF heterostructures. Figure 1a is a typical SEM image of resultant $\mathsf{\Lambda}_{\mathsf{c}}\mathsf{M}\mathsf{I}\mathsf{L}\mathsf{-}125\ @\mathsf{Z}\mathsf{I}\mathsf{F}\mathsf{-}8,$ demonstrating a unique and uniform morphology. Figure 1b is a magnified SEM image showing that cubic structured ZIF-8 nanoparticles (diameters of ${\sim}200\mathsf{n m}$ ) are selectively adhered on the four corners of $_\mathrm{cMl}\mathrm{_{\ell-1}}25$ with good mechanical robustness, which is evidenced by the well-maintained structure after sonication (Figure S3). As can be seen, the grown ZIF-8 nanoparticles have particular facets oriented to the host $\mathtt{c M l L-125}$ , as marked by red dashed lines. Figure 1c and S4 are TEM images and show the projection of ZIF-8 nanoparticles with the host $\mathtt{c M l L-}125$ , in which the ZIF-8 projections are well faceted with the host. Figure 1d is a high-angle annular darkfield image taken under the scanning transmission electron microscopy mode (HAADF-STEM), and shows much brighter contrast of ZIF-8 nanoparticles. This indicates that the ZIF-8 nanoparticles grown on the corners contain relatively heavier elements than the host. Figure 1e are elemental maps of Zn and Ti, from which ZIF-8 containing Zn on four corners and the host $\mathtt{c M l L-125}$ containing Ti can be seen. Figure 1f is a superimposed elemental map showing the composition distribution. In the $c M I L-125@Z I F-8$ heterostructure, four Zn-enriched faceted nanoparticles are preferentially formed on the corners of Ti-enriched $\mathsf{c M l L-}125$ host.
Inductive coupled plasma optical emission spectrometer (ICP-OES) was employed to determine the contents of ZIF-8 and $\mathtt{c M l L-}125$ in $c M I L-125@Z I F-8$ , which are calculated as $\sim30.6\%$ and $69.4\%$ based on the $\pi i/Z n$ mass ratio $(\sim1.83/1)$ . Figure S5 is a Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectra of $\mathsf{c M I L-}125@21\mathsf{F}{\cdot}8$ , in which the bands at $2929\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ , $1379\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ and $1572\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ are assigned to the $C H_{3}^{-}$ group of 2-MeIM in ZIF-8 and carboxyl group of ATA in $\mathtt{c M l L-}125$ , respectively. Figure 2a is the XRD pattern of $c111.125@215.8$ and shows both $\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathrm{c}}\mathsf{M l L}.$ 125 and ZIF-8 diffraction peaks. The $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption isotherms (Figure S6) of ZIF-8, $\mathtt{c M l L-125}$ and $\mathsf{c M I L}{-}125@21\mathsf{F}{-}8$ demonstrate their microporous structures. The BET surface areas and pore volumes are illustrated in Table S1.
To understand the formation mechanism of $c M I L-125@Z I F-8$ heterostructures, we investigated intermediate steps during the formation of $c M I L-125@Z I F-8$ heterostructures. Figure 1g is a typical TEM image taken from a host $\mathsf{c M l L-}125$ nanoparticle, and Figure 1h is the corresponding selected area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern. By corelating Figure 1g and 1h, the side facets of the $\mathrm{\Phi_{c}M l{-}}$ 125 can be assigned as {100} planes, and the corner sides as $\{110\}$ planes. The SAED pattern (Figure S7) of $c M I L-125@Z I F-8$ shows only the diffraction spots corresponding to MIL-125. The absence of diffraction spots of ZIF-8 may be ascribed to that the crystalline structure of ZIF materials was easily damaged and converted into amorphous structure under irradiation of electron beam.46, 47 Figure 1i and 1j are SEM images taken from initial growth of ZIF-8 on the cake-like $\mathsf{c M l L-}125$ host (products collected after 2 min of ZIF-8 growth). Some nucleates are clearly seen only at the corner of the host $\mathtt{c M l L-125}$ , rather than on the side or top surface, indicating that only {110} surfaces of MIL-125 are the nucleation sites for growing ZIF-8.
To further study the formation of ZIF-8 on $\mathsf{c M l L-}125$ , the samples after 2 min of ZIF-8 growth in the presence of $\mathsf{c M l L-}125$ as the substrate were directly observed by SEM without centrifugation and methanol washing treatment, showing nucleation of ZIF-8 on the corner of $\mathtt{c M l L-}125$ , while ZIF-8 nanocrystals not connected to $\mathsf{c M l L}$ 125 formed through homogeneous nucleation were not observed (Figure S8). Then, $\mathsf{c M l L-}125$ were added after homogenous nucleation and growth of ZIF-8 for 2 and 5 min. The collected sample at 2 min showed the co-existence of dodecahedral ZIF-8 particles with an average diameter of ${\sim}820\ \mathsf{n m}$ , presumably generated by homogeneous nucleation and growth of ZIF-8 itself, and small ZIF-8 nanocrystals with open holes, possibly through heterogeneous nucleation on the corners of $\mathsf{c M l L-}125$ and then detached (Figure S9). When the pre-nucleation time of ZIF-8 prolonged to $5\min$ , only a mixture of dodecahedral ZIF-8 particles and $\mathsf{c M l L-}125$ ViweawsAroticblseeOrnvliende (Figure S10). These results indicate the growDthOIo: f10.M10IL3-91/2D50S@CZ0I0F-4817iKs through heterogeneous nucleation of ZIF-8 clusters on {110} facets of MIL-125. However, if $\mathsf{c M l L-}125$ templates were added after the homogeneous nucleation of ZIF-8, the probability of homogenous nucleation and growth of ZIF-8 is increased, leading to a mixture of products.
Without the assistance of surfactants, it has been reported that the crystal growth of ZIF-8 starts with formation of cubes exposing six {001} facets,48, 49 from which thermodynamically more stable rhombic dodecahedra with twelve {110} facets are finally obtained. Consistent with the literature, in our experiments the direct growth of ZIF-8 in methanol without $\mathtt{c M l L-125}$ host resulted in the formation of ZIF-8 nanoparticles with a dodecahedral morphology, as shown in Figure S11, 12. In contrast, with $\mathsf{c M l L-}125$ as host and its exposed {110} surfaces, cubic structured ZIF-8 may be epitaxially grown on the {110} surfaces of $\mathsf{c M l L-125}$ due to their small lattice mismatch in the $\{001\}_{\tt Z I F-8}/\{110\}_{\tt c M1L-125}$ interface. The epitaxial relationship of $\{100\}_{\mathrm{ZIF}}.$ $\mathsf{\Omega}_{8}//\{001\}_{\mathsf{c m i L-}125}$ and $\smash{\{010\}_{21\ensuremath{\mathbb{F}}-8}//\{110\}_{\mathrm{cM}1\ensuremath{\mathrm{L}}-125}}$ (proposed in Scheme 1C) can be evidenced by SEM and TEM (Figure 1b,c), indicating the growth behavior of ZIF-8 crystals is manipulated due to the existence of {110} surfaces of $\mathsf{c}^{\mathsf{M}|\mathsf{L}-125}$ .
